# Single source of truth for settings (R3)

Several binaries historically grew **two parallel stores**: a
product-specific JSON file
(`%PROGRAMDATA%\AIC\OfflinePimAgent\settings.json`,
`/etc/aic/pim-offline-agent/settings.json`) used by the legacy import
path, and the `pim-app-config` platform store (HKLM under
`HKLM\Software\AnalogInfo\<program_id>`; libsecret / Keychain plus
`/etc/aic/<program_id>/settings.toml` elsewhere) used by the
configurator GUIs and the manifest-driven flow.

When a binary reads both, "effective" configuration becomes a function
of read order, file mtime, and whether the configurator ran last. The
audit caught an agent reading its old JSON **after** the operator had
updated the same key in HKLM, silently reverting the change.

## The rule

> **Every binary names exactly ONE canonical settings store in its
> `app-config.toml` manifest. Reads at runtime resolve through that
> store and only that store. Any second store that historically held the
> same data is a migration source — read once on startup if the
> canonical store has not been initialized for this machine, then
> written through to the canonical store and never read again. Writes
> ALWAYS go to the canonical store.**

1. **Every manifest declares its canonical store.** Today it is implicit
   in the `pim-config-storage` backend selection per OS (registry on
   Windows, libsecret + TOML elsewhere). When the typed field ships, the
   manifest names it explicitly.
2. **The startup migration is one-shot.** On first boot after upgrade,
   if the canonical store has no rows for this machine AND the legacy
   store does, copy each value through `AppConfig::set_value` (so R2
   validation runs), then rename the legacy file to
   `<name>.migrated-<ISO-8601-UTC>.json` (Unix) or set a `Migrated`
   `REG_DWORD = 1` sentinel under the legacy key (Windows). Later boots
   see the sentinel and skip entirely.
3. **The migration runs through the manifest gate.** A legacy key the
   new manifest does not declare produces a `warn!` naming the orphan
   (no values) and stays in the legacy file for the operator. It is
   neither silently dropped nor written to the canonical store.
4. **Writes ALWAYS go to the canonical store.** No path may write the
   legacy file after migration. Legacy `Settings::save_to_file` helpers
   are removed or renamed
   `save_to_legacy_file_for_export_only` so intent is unambiguous.

## Per-binary canonical store

| Binary | Canonical store | Legacy migration source (one-shot) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `pim-offline-server` | `pim-app-config` platform store, program id `pim-offline-server` | none — greenfield |
| `pim-offline-agent` | `pim-app-config` platform store, program id `pim-offline-agent` | server-produced `settings.json` imported via `pim-offline-agent import-settings`; **NOT** read at runtime |
| `pim-offline-client-configurator-{win32,tauri}` | same as `pim-offline-agent` (edits the agent's store) | n/a |
| `pim-offline-server-configurator-{win32,tauri}` | same as `pim-offline-server` | n/a |
| `pim-offline-client-elevate-{win32,tauri}` | none (reads pipe path only) | n/a |
| `pim-app-config` (sample CLI) | the manifest passed via `--manifest` | n/a |

The agent does **not** read `settings.json` at runtime — the import is a
one-shot translation into the canonical platform store. This rule is the
authoritative statement of that contract; per-platform paths live in
`pim-app-config/docs/CONFIGURATION_STORAGE.md`.

## Forbidden constructs

- A runtime resolver that reads `settings.json` directly (without going
  through `AppConfig::get_value`). Post-migration that file is an import
  payload, not a configuration source.
- A `Settings::save(&self)` helper that writes the legacy file after
  migration. Delete it or rename it
  `save_to_legacy_file_for_export_only(&self, &Path)` with a doc comment.
- A configurator that writes to two stores ("HKLM AND the file because
  we used to"). Write only the canonical store; the daemon picks it up
  on the next restart or hot reload.
- Per-read fallback chains (`read HKLM → fall back to settings.json →
  fall back to manifest default`) **at runtime**. The legacy fallback is
  the one-shot startup migration, not a per-request resolution step.

## Worked violation → fix

**Violation:** `load()` reads the HKLM snapshot, then reads the JSON
file, then merges one over the other. The operator's configurator edit
is silently reverted or silently honored depending on history.

**Fix:**

```rust
pub fn load() -> Result<ResolvedSettings> {
    let cfg = AppConfig::for_program("pim-offline-agent")?;
    Self::migrate_from_legacy_once(&cfg)?;   // no-op once the sentinel is set
    Ok(cfg.snapshot()?)                      // exactly ONE store
}
```

`migrate_from_legacy_once` returns early on
`cfg.has_been_initialized()?`, treats an unreadable legacy file as empty
(with a `warn!`), writes each pair via `cfg.set_value` while logging
`UnknownKey` orphans without aborting, renames the legacy file, and
calls `cfg.mark_initialized()`.

